  "problem": "Which of the following values of $t$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{5t}{2}\\ge 15\\,?$\n\nI. $t=4$\n\nII. $t=6$\n\nIII. $t=8$",
  "worked_solution": "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=4$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 4}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{20}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ 10 & \\ge 15\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=4$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=4$ ** does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=6$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 6}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{30}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ 15 & \\ge 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=6$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=6$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=8$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 8}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{40}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ 20 & \\ge 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=8$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=8$ **satisfies** this inequality.",
